<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Savoy</h1><p class="st-meta">1003 CE – 1416 CE (approx.) · capital Chambéry</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Alpine county that controlled the Mont Cenis and Great St Bernard passes, the cradle of the house of Savoy and of later Italian kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy">Humbert White-Hands</a>, a noble of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles</a>, held counties around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Savoy</a> from around 1003. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-count-of-savoy">Odo</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa">Adelaide of Turin</a>, bringing lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, so the dynasty straddled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> from its beginnings.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts drew their power from controlling the main passes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and from tolls on the trade that crossed them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-count-of-savoy">Peter II</a>, called the 'little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>', won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> in the 13th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> became the capital in 1295. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-vi-count-of-savoy">Amadeus VI</a>, the Green Count, led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> in 1366, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-vii-count-of-savoy">Amadeus VII</a> acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> in 1388.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1416 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Sigismund</a> raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy">Amadeus VIII</a> to the rank of duke, and the county became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy</a>. The dynasty went on to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and, from 1861, all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-savoy.jpg" alt="County of Savoy" width="320" height="436"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon /ˈæɫfə.θɒn/ (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:County_of_Savoy_locator_map_(1250).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1003 CE – 1416 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chambéry</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Humbert White-Hands, Peter II of Savoy, Amadeus VI of Savoy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Franco-Provençal</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France, Italy, Switzerland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Savoy</dd></dl></aside></div>
